     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Bactericidal beta-lactams (mech unknown, perhaps activating autolytic enzymes that destroy the cell wall). Clavulanate or sulbactam extends amoxicillin/ampicillin to MSSA, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis, Bacteroides, E. coli, K. pneumoniae."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Bronchiectasis exacerbation w.o prior cultures: amoxicillin/clavulanate, azithro, clarithromycin or doxycycline."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Adult LRTI:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " amoxicillin 875 mg q12h or 500 mg q8h; amoxicillin/clavulanate 875 mg q12h or 500 mg q8h (take @ the start of a meal). Ampicillin/sulbactam 1.5–3 g IV every six hrs. Reduce most penicillins in severe renal insuff (⍉antistaphylococcal ones)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Mostly hypersensitivity:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " immediate (anaphylaxis, urticaria, angioedema) or delayed (serum sickness, rashes, exfoliative derm). Ampicillin/amoxicillin rash, and a ⍉allergic maculopapular rash in infectious mononucleosis (days 4–7). ↑doses: seizures (esp. renal insuff); nephritis, C. difficile diarrhea, Coombs+ hemolytic anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia. Amox-clav and amp-sulbactam: hepatitis/cholestatic jaundice (USU reversible)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Serious prior allergic rxn to a penicillin (or other beta-lactam, per the amox-clav and amp-sulbactam labels). Amox-clav/amp-sulbactam: prior cholestatic jaundice/hepatic dysfun w that drug. Most pts who report penicillin allergy don't rxt on re-exposure; w a vague or inconsistent Hx and ⍉effective or convenient alt, skin testing may be done."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
